    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>As regent from 1916 and emperor from 1930, Haile Selassie pursued a cautious modernisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, with a written constitution in 1931 and a revised one in 1955, against the steady resistance of the landed nobility.</li><li>Driven into exile by the Italian invasion of 1935–1936, he pleaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s case before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> in 1941, and later presided over the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>.</li><li>His government has been criticised as autocratic, famine and unrest eroded its support, and in 1974 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> deposed him; he was killed in its custody in 1975.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On his 1924 tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Tafari travelled with a pride of lions, which he gave as gifts to the president and prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>. In return for two lions the United Kingdom handed back the imperial crown of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a>, taken in 1868.</p></aside>
    </section>

