    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1892</span><span class="rf-text">Tafari Makonnen is born on 23 July in the village of Ejersa Goro, in the Hararghe province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a> is deposed on 27 September, Tafari is proclaimed Crown Prince and Regent Plenipotentiary under Empress Zewditu</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text">Following the death of Empress Zewditu he is crowned emperor on 2 November at the Cathedral of St. George in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, taking the regnal name Haile Selassie I</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1931</span><span class="rf-text">He introduces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia">first written constitution</a> on 16 July, providing for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicameral-legislature">bicameral legislature</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Defeated at the Battle of Maychew, he leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> on 2 May and addresses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> on the Italian invasion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">He enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on 5 May, exactly five years after the Italian forces had entered the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">He presides over the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> and is elected its first chairperson</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1974</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, a committee of junior officers and enlisted men, deposes the 82-year-old emperor on 12 September</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menelik-ii.jpg" alt="Menelik II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menelik_II_in_coronation_garb,_Emperor_of_Ethiopia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Haile Selassie was born Tafari Makonnen on 23 July 1892 in the village of Ejersa Goro, in the Hararghe province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. As a child he was known as Lij Tafari Makonnen; the name Haile Selassie, "Power of the Trinity" in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, was given to him at his infant baptism. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Makonnen Wolde Mikael, was a grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> of Shewa and a general of the First Italo–Ethiopian War who had a large part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a>. His mother, Woizero Yeshimebet Ali Abba Jifar, was the daughter of a ruling chief from Were Ilu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo">Wollo</a> province. Through his paternal grandmother, an aunt of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, he belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>, whose tradition traces every monarch to Menelik I, described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a> as the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>.</p><p>Ras Makonnen arranged for Tafari and his first cousin Imru Haile Selassie to be taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> by Abba Samuel Wolde Kahin, an Ethiopian Capuchin friar, and by Dr. Vitalien, a surgeon from Guadeloupe. On 1 November 1905, at the age of 13, Tafari was named Dejazmach, a title roughly equivalent to count. His father died at Kulibi in 1906. Tafari assumed the titular governorship of Selale in 1906, was appointed governor over part of Sidamo in 1907, and was made governor of Harar in 1910 or 1911. It is alleged that in his late teens he was married to Woizero Altayech and that his daughter Princess Romanework was born of this union. On 3 August 1911 he married Menen Asfaw of Ambassel, niece of the heir to the throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a>; the marriage lasted 50 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iyasu-v.jpg" alt="Iyasu V" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Iyasu V</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lidj_Iyasu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a>, the designated but uncrowned emperor from 1913, offended the nobles of the court, and his flirtation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was considered treasonous by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a> leadership. On 27 September 1916 he was deposed on the grounds of conversion to Islam; how far Tafari contributed to the movement has been much discussed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>'s daughter was named Empress Zewditu, and Tafari, who drew support from both progressive and conservative factions, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>, Crown Prince and Regent Plenipotentiary. On 27 October an army loyal to them defeated and captured Iyasu's father, Negus Mikael of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo">Wollo</a>, at the Battle of Segale. Tafari's position was weak at first, but Zewditu's authority gradually declined while his grew.</p><p>As regent he continued the cautious modernisation begun by Menelik II. He secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1923 by promising to eradicate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and in 1924 toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. In 1928 Dejazmach Balcha Safo, governor of Sidamo, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> with a large army; Tafari had the army bought off and replaced him. After a failed palace coup that September, the Empress crowned Tafari Negus, or king, on 7 October 1928. Conservatives saw two monarchs in one capital as an insult to the crown, and her husband, Ras Gugsa Welle, marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>; he was defeated and killed at the Battle of Anchem on 31 March 1930. Zewditu died on 2 April 1930, of paratyphoid fever and complications from diabetes, and Tafari was crowned emperor as Haile Selassie I on 2 November 1930.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 16 July 1931 Haile Selassie introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia">first written constitution</a>, which provided for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicameral-legislature">bicameral legislature</a>, kept power with the nobility and limited the succession to his own descendants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded in early October 1935, with a modern army, a large air force and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>. After the northern armies were destroyed he led the last of them at the Battle of Maychew on 31 March 1936 and was defeated. His council agreed that he should leave to put Ethiopia's case to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>; he departed on 2 May 1936 and told the assembly that Italy was using chemical weapons on military and civilian targets alike. The League agreed only to partial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. He spent the years 1936 to 1941 at Fairfield House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>On 18 January 1941 he crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> into Ethiopia, and on 5 May 1941 he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, urging his people not to return the atrocities they had suffered. On 27 August 1942 he confirmed the legal basis for the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, with severe penalties for slave trading. Ethiopia became a charter member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and in 1950 the General Assembly federated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> with it. Between 1941 and 1959 he worked for the autocephaly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a> Tewahedo Church, granted in 1959. A revised constitution of 1955 made the lower house of parliament an elected body while he retained effective power. In 1963 he presided over the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>, with its headquarters in Addis Ababa, and was elected its first chairperson.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The landed aristocracy consistently opposed his changes. A progressive tax scheme of 1942 failed against the nobility, and where new land taxes were imposed landowners often passed the burden on to the peasants. A single progressive income tax in 1966 provoked a revolt in Gojjam, which was repressed, though enforcement of the tax was abandoned. His decision to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1936 was itself contested: the nobleman Blatta Tekle Wolde Hawariat objected strenuously to a monarch fleeing before an invader. On 13 December 1960, while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, the imperial guard staged a coup and proclaimed his elder son, Asfa Wossen, emperor; the army and police crushed it, but it had support among students and the educated classes.</p><p>Critics have called his administration autocratic and illiberal, and the 1955 constitution has been criticised for reasserting "the indisputable power of the monarch". In 1948 a rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> was met with a violent crackdown, and many Hararis left; the historians Tim Carmichael and Roman Loimeier hold that he was directly involved in its suppression. According to some sources the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> language was de facto banned from education and administration late in his reign. A famine in Tigray from 1958 is estimated to have killed approximately 100,000 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s federation was dissolved and the territory made a province in 1962, and the Imperial Army committed atrocities in the war against Eritrean separatists. In his last years Freedom House rated Ethiopia "Not Free", and dissidents were imprisoned and tortured.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 1970s student unrest was a regular feature of Ethiopian life, and conservatives at court, in parliament and in the church blocked his proposals for land reform. Famine, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo">Wollo</a>, is estimated to have killed 40,000 to 80,000 Ethiopians between 1972 and 1974; some reports suggest that the Emperor was unaware of its extent, while others assert that he knew of it. In February 1974 four days of riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> left five dead. The army mutinied, beginning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>, the prime minister, Aklilu Habte-Wold, resigned on 27 February, and a four-day general strike followed in March.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, a group of junior officers and enlisted men set up in June to investigate the military's demands, deposed the 82-year-old emperor on 12 September 1974. He was held briefly at the 4th Army Division and then under house arrest in the palace. On 23 November 60 former high officials, among them his grandson Rear Admiral Iskinder Desta, were executed by firing squad, and the Derg announced the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. State media reported that he had died on 27 August 1975 of respiratory failure after a prostate operation, an account that Asrat Woldeyes rejected. In 1994, three years after the Derg fell, an Ethiopian court found former officers guilty of strangling him in his bed on the regime's orders. His bones had been found under a concrete slab on the palace grounds in 1992, and on 5 November 2000 he was given a funeral at the Holy Trinity Cathedral in Addis Ababa, which the government declined to make an official imperial one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Haile Selassie is widely considered a defining figure in modern Ethiopian history, and his legacy remains a topic of debate. He helped to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> University and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>, the precursor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>. His 1936 speech made him an icon for anti-fascists around the world, and he is remembered as a prominent anti-colonial leader. In the 1950s he was widely regarded as a modernising and capable ruler; in the 1970s famine and economic turmoil damaged that reputation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> accused him of hoarding millions in Swiss banks, and Ethiopian media in 1974 claimed a fortune of 11 billion dollars, though records put his net worth at £22,000 as late as 1959. In recent years there has been a resurgence of nostalgia for his reign and of support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, a religion that emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in the 1930s after news of his coronation arrived there, he is accorded divine importance; among adherents he is called the returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the messiah who will lead the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and its diaspora to freedom. He was himself an adherent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a>. When he visited Jamaica on 21 April 1966, approximately one hundred thousand Rastafari met him at the airport, a day commemorated as Grounation Day. In a 1967 interview he denied his alleged divinity, though many Rastafari do not read it as a denial. Most Rastafari rejected the funeral of 2000 and did not accept that the bones were his.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Haile Selassie I ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as regent from 1916 and as emperor from 1930 to 1974. He resisted the Italian invasion, pleaded his country's case before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, returned from exile in 1941 and helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>. Deposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> in 1974 and killed in its custody in 1975, he is held to be divine by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>.</em></p>

